This test exercises the morpheme stacking mechanism borrowed from Ithkuil's morphological slot system. Any term can carry a chain of suffixes selecting aspect, modality, certainty, and scope values, with each slot drawn from a fixed vocabulary.

The aspect slot carries tense-and-phase information. The document shows terms with progressive aspect meaning something is in progress, perfective aspect meaning something has completed, iterative aspect meaning something is repeated, inchoative aspect meaning something is beginning, and terminative aspect meaning something is ending.

The modality slot carries obligation or permission information. The document shows terms marked as must meaning obligatory, may meaning permitted, or cant meaning impossible.

The certainty slot carries epistemic information. The document shows terms marked as certain, probable, or possible.

The scope slot carries scope-of-effect information. The document shows terms marked as local, global, or context-dependent.

Combinations of two morpheme slots begin to convey richer meaning. A term that is progressive and certain means something is definitely happening now. A term that is inchoative and obligatory means something must begin. A term that is iterative and permitted means something may happen repeatedly.

Three-slot stacks express more nuance. Progressive-probable-local means something is probably happening now in a local scope. Perfective-obligatory-global means something must have completed, at global scope.

Four-slot stacks approach the density limit of the notation. Iterative-permitted-doubtful-contextual means something might repeatedly happen, doubtfully, depending on context.

Five-slot stacks are the maximum the notation supports. Progressive-permitted-possibly-local means something may possibly be happening now in a local scope. In English this concept takes nine words, while in CSLv3 it is six tokens.
